        "content": "A noun is a word used to identify a person, place, animal, thing, quality, or idea.\n\n• Proper Nouns: Names of specific individual persons, places, days, months, and geographical features. They ALWAYS begin with a capital letter.\n  Examples: Kwame Nkrumah, Accra, Lake Bosomtwe, Mount Afadjato, Tuesday, September.\n• Common Nouns: General names for a class of persons, places, or items.\n  Examples: doctor, school, classroom, football, river, town.\n• Collective Nouns: Singular names used to describe a whole group or collection of individuals or objects regarded as a single unit.\n  Examples: a pride of lions, a flock of sheep, a fleet of ships, a pack of wolves, a swarm of bees, a choir of singers, a panel of judges, a bouquet of flowers.\n• Abstract Nouns: Names of qualities, concepts, states, emotions, or conditions that have no physical existence and cannot be touched, seen, or tasted.\n  Examples: integrity, courage, sorrow, wisdom, poverty, freedom, patience, honesty.\n• Countable vs Uncountable Nouns:\n  - Countable Nouns: Items that can be counted as individual units. They have singular and plural forms (e.g., one mango, three mangoes; desk/desks; pupil/pupils).\n  - Uncountable (Mass) Nouns: Substances, materials, or concepts that cannot be counted individually. They do not take the indefinite article 'a/an' and have NO plural forms. (e.g., water, air, information, furniture, sand, rice, baggage, equipment, luggage).",
