        "title": "3. Pronouns and Case Functions",
        "content": "A pronoun is a word used in place of a noun or noun phrase to prevent repetitive writing.\n\n• Personal Pronouns & Case:\n  - Subjective Case (performs the action): I, you, he, she, it, we, they.\n  - Objective Case (receives the action or follows a preposition): me, you, him, her, it, us, them.\n  - Possessive Determiners: my, your, his, her, its, our, their.\n  - Possessive Pronouns: mine, yours, his, hers, ours, theirs (Notice: NO apostrophe in hers, yours, theirs, its!).\n\n• The Object of a Preposition Rule:\nWhenever a pronoun follows a preposition (such as between, to, for, with, except, behind), it MUST be in the objective case.\n  - Correct: Between you and me (NOT 'between you and I').\n  - Correct: The teacher gave the books to Kofi and her (NOT 'to Kofi and she').\n\n• Relative Pronouns:\n  - 'Who' refers to people as subjects: \"The student who won the award...\"\n  - 'Whom' refers to people as objects: \"The prefect whom we elected...\"\n  - 'Which' refers to animals and inanimate things: \"The car which broke down...\"\n  - 'Whose' indicates possession: \"The girl whose bag was stolen...\"\n\n• Reflexive Pronouns:\nmyself, yourself, himself, herself, itself, ourselves, yourselves, themselves.\nCaution: Non-standard forms like 'theirselves', 'hisself', or 'ourself' DO NOT exist in standard English.",
