        "title": "3. The Golden Rules of Subject-Verb Concord",
        "content": "Concord means grammatical agreement between the subject and the verb in number (singular/plural) and person.\n\n• Golden Rule 1: A singular subject requires a singular verb (singular verbs in the simple present end in '-s' or '-es'). A plural subject requires a plural verb.\n  - Singular: \"The teacher writes on the chalkboard.\"\n  - Plural: \"The teachers write on the chalkboard.\"\n\n• Golden Rule 2: Two or more singular subjects joined by 'and' take a plural verb.\n  - \"Kofi and Ama are going to the library.\"\n  Exception: When two words joined by 'and' represent a single compound entity or dish, take a singular verb:\n  - \"Bread and butter is a quick breakfast.\"\n  - \"Gari and beans is my favorite lunch.\"\n\n• Golden Rule 3: Intervening Expressions Rule:\nPhrases such as 'as well as', 'together with', 'along with', 'in addition to', 'accompanied by', and 'including' do NOT alter the number of the subject. Look only at the main subject preceding the phrase!\n  - \"The headmaster, together with all the teachers, IS attending the conference.\"\n  - \"The players, as well as the coach, ARE celebrating the victory.\"",
