        "content": "The comma [,] indicates a short pause and organizes grammatical structures within a sentence.\n\n• Rule 1: Separating Items in a List or Series:\n  Use commas to separate three or more words or phrases in a list.\n  - \"We bought ripe plantains, cassava, fresh fish, and palm oil at the market.\"\n\n• Rule 2: After Introductory Dependent Clauses and Phrases:\n  When a dependent adverbial clause or long introductory phrase begins a sentence, place a comma before the independent main clause.\n  - \"Although it rained heavily, all the students arrived on time.\"\n  - \"After cleaning the science laboratory, we returned to our classroom.\"\n\n• Rule 3: Setting Off Parenthetical Information (Appositives):\n  An appositive is a noun or phrase that renames or gives extra information about a noun. It is bracketed by commas on both sides.\n  - \"Mr. Mensah, our dedicated mathematics teacher, won the district award.\"\n\n• Rule 4: Before Coordinating Conjunctions in Compound Sentences:\n  Place a comma before coordinating conjunctions (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so - FANBOYS) when joining two independent clauses.\n  - \"The whistle blew, and the football match commenced.\"\n\n• Rule 5: Direct Address and Salutations:\n  - \"Kofi, please open the window.\"\n  - \"Dear Auntie Mary,\"",
        "keyTakeaway": "Always place a comma after an introductory dependent clause: 'If you study hard, you will pass.'",
