        "title": "3. The Apostrophe: Possession vs Contraction",
        "content": "The apostrophe ['] performs two primary duties: showing possession (ownership) and marking missing letters in contractions.\n\n• Singular Noun Possession: Add apostrophe + 's' ('s).\n  - The bag belonging to the boy → The boy's bag.\n  - The car belonging to James → James's car (or James' car).\n\n• Plural Noun Possession:\n  - If the plural ends in 's', add ONLY the apostrophe at the end:\n    * The school for boys → The boys' school.\n    * The dormitory for girls → The girls' dormitory.\n  - If the plural noun is irregular and does NOT end in 's', add apostrophe + 's':\n    * The toys belonging to children → The children's toys.\n    * The clinic for women → The women's clinic.\n\n• Contractions (Omission of Letters):\n  - do not → don't; cannot → can't; they have → they've; it is → it's.\n\n• The Fatal BECE Confusion: 'It's' vs 'Its':\n  - IT'S (with apostrophe) is a contraction for 'it is' or 'it has'.\n    \"It's raining outside.\" (= It is raining).\n  - ITS (without apostrophe) is a possessive pronoun indicating ownership by an animal or object.\n    \"The cat licked its paws.\" (No apostrophe!).\n    \"Ghana is famous for its rich cultural heritage.\"",
